// case: capitalised-type-parameter
// what: `Float` is not a type either (n30 is the local-binding half), and here
//      the program IS rejected -- so this is the one place in the family where
//      a diagnostic exists, and it points at the wrong thing. Nothing is wrong
//      with the return statement; what is wrong is the parameter annotation
//      that the front end accepted and then carried as a type name. The
//      rejection only happens because the bogus name later reaches the return
//      check and mismatches `float`, and the message names the return.
//
//      It is pinned deliberately, and the pin is wrong on purpose: the header
//      asserts the misleading text, so the day someone teaches the front end
//      that an unknown type name is an error at the annotation, this gate fails
//      and the diagnostic has to be re-declared. FINDINGS F-0011.
// expect-error: expected float, got Float
fn f(x: Float) -> float { return x }

fn main() -> int {
    print(f(1.5))
    return 0
}
